- **Translation File Structure**
  ```
  # Shared package
  common/
  └── locales/
      ├── en/
      │   └── common.json     # Strings reused across projects (auth, common, etc.)
      └── es/
          └── common.json

  # Web-app (Next.js SPA)
  webapp/src/
  ├── locales/
  │   ├── en/
  │   │   ├── payment.json    # Payment page only
  │   │   └── upload.json     # Feature-specific namespace
  │   └── es/
  │       ├── payment.json
  │       └── upload.json
  └── i18n.ts                 # app-specific i18n config

  # Marketing site
  website/src/
  └── locales/
      ├── en/
      │   └── hero.json       # Landing-page copy only
      └── es/
          └── hero.json

  types/i18next.d.ts          # Root-level type declarations (maps all namespaces)
  ```

- **Database Translation Patterns**
  - **Direct Label Translation Object**
    ```typescript
    // ✅ DO: Use direct label translations for simple cases
    interface TableRow {
      id: string;
      label: string; // Default language (en)
      translations: {
        [lang: string]: {
          label: string;
        }
      };
    }

    // Example data
    {
      id: "item-1",
      label: "Blue Background",
      translations: {
        es: {
          label: "Fondo Azul"
        }
        // Add languages as needed
      }
    }
    ```

  - **Multiple Translatable Fields**
    ```typescript
    // ✅ DO: Use translations object for multiple fields
    interface TableRow {
      id: string;
      name: string;  // Default language (en)
      description: string;
      translations?: {
        [lang: string]: {
          name: string;
          description: string;
        }
      }
    }

    // Example data
    {
      id: "item-1",
      name: "Blue Background",
      description: "A solid blue backdrop",
      translations: {
        es: {
          name: "Fondo Azul",
          description: "Un fondo azul sólido"
        }
        // Add languages as needed
      }
    }
    ```

  - **When to Use Each Pattern**
    ```typescript
    // ✅ DO: Use direct label object when:
    - Only one field needs translation
    - All languages are required
    - UI needs quick access to translations
    - Performance is critical

    // ✅ DO: Use translations object when:
    - Multiple fields need translation
    - Languages are optional
    - Separating content from translations
    - Future fields might need translation
    ```

- **Translation Key Naming**
  ```typescript
  // ✅ DO: Use nested objects for better organization
  {
    "feature": {
      "section": {
        "key": "value"
      }
    }
  }

  // ❌ DON'T: Use flat keys with repetitive prefixes
  {
    "feature_section_key": "value"
  }
  ```

- **Component Usage**
  ```typescript
  // ✅ DO: Use namespaced translations
  const { t } = useTranslation('payment');
  t('success.title');

  // ✅ DO: Use multiple namespaces when needed
  const { t } = useTranslation(['payment', 'common']);
  t('payment:success.title');
  t('common:error.message');

  // ❌ DON'T: Use the default namespace without specifying
  const { t } = useTranslation();
  t('success.title'); // Unclear which namespace
  ```

- **Translation File Organization**
  - **Common Translations (`common.json`)**
    ```json
    {
      "error": {
        "title": "Error",
        "description": "An error occurred"
      },
      "loading": "Loading...",
      "buttons": {
        "save": "Save",
        "cancel": "Cancel"
      }
    }
    ```
  - **Feature Translations (`feature.json`)**
    ```json
    {
      "title": "Feature Title",
      "description": "Feature description",
      "sections": {
        "main": {
          "title": "Section Title"
        }
      }
    }
    ```

- **Type Safety**
  ```typescript
  // ✅ DO: Define types for translations
  // types/i18next.d.ts
  import 'i18next';
  declare module 'i18next' {
    interface CustomTypeOptions {
      defaultNS: 'common';
      resources: {
        common: typeof import('../locales/en/common.json');
        payment: typeof import('../locales/en/payment.json');
      }
    }
  }

  // ❌ DON'T: Use translations without type checking
  const { t } = useTranslation();
  t('nonexistent.key'); // Should show type error
  ```

- **Database Translation Types**
  ```typescript
  // ✅ DO: Define clear interfaces for database translations
  // Direct label pattern
  interface TranslatedLabel {
    [lang: string]: string;
  }

  // Translations object pattern
  interface Translations {
    [lang: string]: {
      [field: string]: string;
    }
  }

  // ✅ DO: Use discriminated unions for different patterns
  type TranslationField = 
    | { label: TranslatedLabel }
    | { translations: Translations }
  ```

- **Best Practices**
  - Keep translations organized by feature/page
  - Use common namespace for shared translations
  - Always specify namespace when using translations
  - Add new languages in all namespaces simultaneously
  - Use TypeScript for type safety
  - Keep translation keys descriptive and hierarchical
  - Document all namespaces in i18next.d.ts
  - Be consistent with database translation patterns
  - Consider query performance when choosing patterns

- **Adding New Features**
  1. Create translation files in all language directories
  2. Add namespace to i18n.ts configuration
  3. Update type definitions in i18next.d.ts
  4. Use namespaced translations in components
  5. Choose appropriate database translation pattern
  6. Update database schemas and migrations

- **Interpolation**
  ```typescript
  // ✅ DO: Use type-safe interpolation
  t('welcome', { name: user.name });
  
  // ✅ DO: Use pluralization
  t('items', { count: items.length });
  
  // ❌ DON'T: Use string concatenation
  t('welcome') + user.name;
  ```

- **Maintenance**
  - Review unused translations periodically
  - Keep language files in sync
  - Update types when adding/removing translations
  - Use translation management tools for collaboration
  - Consider automated translation verification
  - Monitor database translation coverage
  - Plan for language additions/removals

- **Common Issues**
  - Missing translations in some languages
  - Inconsistent naming across namespaces
  - Type mismatches in interpolation
  - Duplicate translations in different namespaces
  - Unnecessary nesting levels
  - Mixed translation patterns in database
  - Performance impact of translation queries

